`method` on a JavaScript native mark calls the identifier on its first
argument, which is the only shape a DOM API has.

This is the case the flag exists for. Go can express a receiver-first call as a
method expression, so the flag only makes its output idiomatic
(native_method_go.txtar). JavaScript has no such spelling: a
`CanvasRenderingContext2D` method is a property of the context, so
`ctx.arc(x, y, r, s, e)` is the only thing that runs, and before this flag the
only reachable output was `arc(ctx, x, y, r, s, e)` — a call to a global that
does not exist.

Describing a host drawing context in SNGL is what needs it (#213): the shape
overrides call the context's own methods, and the emitted JavaScript is the
same code the platform used to build by hand.

`paint` is called rather than merely declared, because a func nothing calls is
shaken and an archive that shows neither the call nor the declaration asserts
nothing about how either is spelled -- the denies below included.

deny out/none/html/index.html `[^.]\barc\(` -- a bare arc() is a call to a global nobody declared
deny out/none/html/index.html `arc\(c,` -- the receiver belongs before the dot, not in the arguments

-- app.sngl --
import ui "sngl:ui"
import js "sngl:language/js"

output {
    none {
        html
    }
}

#[js.native("CanvasRenderingContext2D")]
struct Ctx {}

#[js.native("arc", "", method)]
func Ctx.arc(x float, y float, r float, start float, end float)

#[js.native("fill", "", method)]
func Ctx.fill()

component main() ui.node {
    var n = 0
    var c Ctx

    func paint(c Ctx) {
        c.arc(1.0, 2.0, 3.0, 0.0, 6.28)
        c.fill()
    }

    ui.button(text="draw", @click {
        n += 1
        paint(c)
    })
}

ui.window {
    main()
}
-- out/none/html/index.html --
